<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birgi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pyrgion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birgi is a small town in İzmir province, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on the slopes of the Bozdağ mountains. Known in Byzantine times as Pyrgion, it became the first capital of the Aydinid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in the early 14th century, and its Great Mosque, built in 1312 for Mehmed Bey, survives. Its Ottoman houses, such as the Çakırağa Mansion, are well preserved.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birgi.jpg" alt="Birgi" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Canel Ölgün · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birgi_de_yanl%C4%B1z_ev.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Birgi lies in the district of Ödemiş in İzmir Province. Its present name derives from its medieval Greek name, Pyrgion, which means Little Tower. In antiquity the town was called Dios Hieron, the Sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, one of two cities of that name, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> with the annexation of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>. It was renamed Christoupolis in the 7th century and was known as Pyrgion from the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Roman city had a Christian bishopric, attested as an episcopal see from at least 451 and subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> until the late 12th century, when it became a metropolis of its own. Four of its early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> are known: Stephen took part in the Council of Ephesus of 431, Zoetus attended the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> of 680 and the Council in Trullo of 692, and a later Stephen of Pyrgion attended the councils of 869–870 and 879–880 that dealt with Patriarch Photios I. Dioshieron survives as a titular see of the Roman Catholic Church that has never been assigned.</p><p>Pyrgion fell to the Turks in 1307 and became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> of Aydin. Ibn Battuta visited the town and attended a lecture by the professor Muhyi al-Din. Birgi was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1390, and the future Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> was educated at its Fortress Madrasa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since the reorganisation of 2013 Birgi has been a neighbourhood of the municipality of Ödemiş; it had 1,832 inhabitants in 2022. Besides the mosque of Mehmet Bey and the Çakırağa Mansion, built in the 18th century by the wealthy Çakırağa family, it keeps the 14th-century Aydınoğlu Baths, the 16th-century tomb and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> of Birgivi Mehmet Efendi, the Sultanşah Mausoleum, the 19th-century Sandıkoğlu Mansion and its city walls. ÇEKÜL has listed it for its Seljuk and Ottoman architecture since 1994, and archaeologists unearthed a Byzantine fort there in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Birgi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mehmed Bey, a commander in the service of the Germiyanids, conquered <b>Birgi</b>, Ayasoluk (Ephesus) and the upper Smyrna citadel from the Byzantines in the first decade of the 14th century and founded his own beylik, with Birgi as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/aydinids">Aydinids</a></li></ul></section>
